disney gets down to business with a live  action remake of their animated mulan  but is it as powerful pleasurable and  indestructible as the original  yipikai movie lovers i'm jan and today  i'm explaining the 14  biggest differences between the new  movie and the 1998 classic  some spoilers ahead so take care if  you've not seen it yet  he may be a fan favorite but that didn't  save the much loved  mooshu from being cut from the new film  dishonor this honor on your whole family  brilliantly voiced by eddie murphy the  travel-sized dragon with a  larger-than-life personality brought a  wealth of memorable moments and  hilarious comic relief to the animated  movie  i am the powerful the pleasurable  the indestructible however his comedic  tone didn't fit the style of the  live-action film which leans more  towards  epic martial arts war movie and  crucially while many western disney fans  love mushu  according to producer jason reed his  silly shenanigans didn't play well to  audiences in china where dragons are  important symbols of power strength and  good fortune  in contrast to western mythology where  they've often been seen as dangerous and  aggressive creatures to be defeated  the new mulan does have a mythological  sidekick of sorts though this  time her guardian is a phoenix a silent  character that guides her on her journey  to duty honor and being true to herself  in chinese tradition the phoenix is  often regarded as a symbol of female  power and together with the dragon the  pair of mythological creatures sit on  either side of the emperor  there are some beautiful shots of the  phoenix appearing at crucial moments in  mulan's story  and although it is sad to lose the  iconic character of mushu his  replacement with the phoenix does better  suit this more serious  version of the tale the remake  introduces several brand new characters  my favorite of these is a sorceress  played by gong lee who's an ally of the  movie's main villain bori khan  she's a powerful and intriguing figure  and the way she shapeshifts into  birds creates some truly arresting  visuals and is also a hat tip to villain  chanu's falcon in the original movie  of course she's much more crucial to the  plot than the 1998 film's bird of prey  with her shape-shifting and magical  ability to take over another human body  proving integral to the villain's  strategy and her character also adds an  interesting parallel with mulan  balancing the protagonist's story as she  knows what it feels like to be  ostracized for being different and  powerful  and her arc enhances the themes of  identity and belonging so crucial to the  film  when it came to mulan's lucky sidekick  qrikey who communicates via chirps  what you mean a loser rather than  cutting him out of the new film entirely  disney took a different  approach keeping his spirits alive by  adding a brand new human character who's  appropriately called cricket  cricket is a fellow army recruit whose  mother says he's a good luck charm  and just like the animated bug  unwittingly got mulan into trouble the  new cricket also accidentally causes a  minor ruckus at the garrison as the new  recruits line up  and like his animated insect counterpart  this cricket also helped mulan out at  some key moments  he's a really sweet new character who  adds some nice comedic touches to the  film  which makes the movies fake out at the  end all the more unbearable  like mushu one character that fans may  find themselves missing is army captain  and love interest lee shang  for the live live-action version his  character has been split in two  with donnie yen playing commander tongue  mulan superior  mentor and actor yosan and taking on the  role of hong koi  a trainee soldier who after a rocky  start sees that there's something  different about mulan compared to the  others and ends up a close ally  according to the film's producer jason  reed the filmmakers felt it was  inappropriate for mulan's commanding  officer to be her love interest in the  remake  so they removed the power differential  by making hong hwa her equal in the army  and similar to the animation there are  some hints at something more between  them  what if she doesn't like me she will  i mean i think she will you know you  never know with women  interestingly the film's hair and makeup  designer has said they went for a young  clean-shaven look that was both feminine  and masculine for hongwei so that mulan  would fit in better when she joins up  and pretends to be male and also because  his character has a fragility to him  when reed was asked whether his  character would be regarded as a  potential lgbtq icon in the way that lee  shang is he said it will play in the  same way as it does in the animated film  as they use that as their model  given the way the live action movie ends  though it feels like the romantic  connection between the two is downplayed  compared to the original  would you like to stay for dinner would  you like to stay forever  dinner would be great the new film does  stick to the main story beats of the  1998 one  but unlike some other disney live-action  adaptations it isn't a shot-for-shot  remake so expect some well-known scenes  to get the chop  for example the animation's iconic scene  of mulan slicing off her long locks to  disguise herself as a soldier  doesn't make it into the live-action  movie for many western audiences cutting  her hair  short was an empowering moment  symbolizing her leaving behind her old  life and its rigid gender-based  expectations  however that moment rang false to  audiences in china as in their culture  male warriors wore their hair long so  the new film takes the animated scene  and gives it a twist  creating its own new empowering moment  for our heroine when she decides to let  her hair loose and willingly reveal her  true identity  this moment comes just after mulan's  one-to-one encounter with the sorceress  who accuses her of hiding who she really  is  your deceit weakens you it poisons your  chi  seeing her tresses flow free as she  fights fiercely on the battlefield as a  moving moment  because mulan has made her own choice to  unmask herself to the world  by contrast in the animation she's  forced out of her male disguise and  unintentionally reveals herself as a  woman  after being injured in battle in the new  version mulan gets to take back her  power  embrace her full identity and make her  own decision as to when it's time to  reveal who she really is  another memorable moment in the animated  movie comes after lee shang tells our  heroine she's not good enough to be a  soldier and orders her to go home  dejected she begins to leave but then  decides to retrieve the arrow from the  pole that everyone has so far  failed to climb using her smarts she  repurposes the heavy weights to help her  scale the pole and reach the arrow  in the live action the poll is gone but  the incredibly difficult task this time  is to walk all the way up an enormous  mountain carrying heavy pails of water  the interesting difference between the  two movies is that in the animation it's  more mulan's ingenuity and determination  that help her succeed in this seemingly  impossible task  whereas in the live action it's her chi  that powers her up the mountain summit  overall the new movie emphasizes the  power of mulan's chi  is making her special which isn't to say  that this mulan isn't smart but it is a  notable difference between the two films  the animations big bad the hun army and  their leader shanyu  have been replaced by bori khan and his  roaring fighters from the north  khan's described by director nikki  carrow as somebody who it looks like  everybody has tried to kill but nobody  has succeeded  and he's played by jason scott lee who  starred in disney live-action  adaptations before  having brought mowgli to life in the  1994 version of the jungle book  another new character introduced in the  latest version is mulan's younger sister  the filmmakers felt it added a broader  emotional context and more motivation  for our heroine especially at the end of  the film  on top of that the differences between  the two sisters helped to highlight just  how different mulan is to other young  women  but adding a new relative to the story  meant the wisecracking grandmother from  the animation who spit in her bean curd  got the boot as they wanted to keep the  focus on woodland's parents  and sister instead in a change from the  animated movie where mulan is already a  young woman at the start  the remake opens with her as a child  this new way of introducing her gives us  some backstory for her character  demonstrating her athleticism and  agility and her strong connection to  achieve from an early age  mulan's striking difference to the other  children in the village and the way the  rest of her neighbors view her  gives us an insight into how society  treats women who don't fit its norms and  the problems it creates for her parents  it is mulan i worry about they'll call  her  witch as producer reed says she has all  these great qualities but she doesn't do  things the way a young girl supposed to  and it isn't until she's dressed as a  boy that people encourage those things  in her  this new opening to the story brings  some comedy to the film as well and also  includes a cool call back to the scene  in the original movie where mulan's dog  little brother helps her with their  chores looking after the chickens  this time though it's a young mulan  herself who energetically chases around  with the chickens and races through the  temple where a pile of fruit takes a  tumble  rather than in the animation where it's  her father's teacup that hits the floor  that fun little sequence does mean  there's no canine character this time  but that's  understandable as this really isn't the  type of disney film where animals  performing household tasks fits in  as for mulan's horse he's got very  similar colouring to her animated horse  khan but given the more realistic feel  of this film don't expect the same  expressive body language or facial  expressions  i'm working on it owen his name in the  new movie is actually black wind because  calling him  khan would be very confusing given the  main villain is named bori khan  in a bold move the creative team behind  mulan chose to move away from the  musical genre in complete contrast to  many other recent disney remakes such as  beauty and the beast aladdin and the  lion king  although the new film does have  fantastical elements it leans more  heavily into epic war territory so the  filmmakers wanted to keep the world  more grounded and realistic and they  felt that mulan bursting into song to  reflect on her situation or the imperial  soldiers busting out a tune while  getting down to business during a  training montage wouldn't set the right  tone  while we don't get any show-stopping  musical numbers we do get some  instrumental tributes to the classic  songs and the movie score and also in  its dialogue  and if you want to know where they all  appear the details are in my mulan  easter eggs and things you missed video  tap here to watch that or follow the  link in the video description  i'll add it here as soon as it's ready  on top of that during the end credits we  hear  two new versions of reflections one by  christina aguilera who also performed  the song for the animation  and one in mandarin by mulan star yifei  plus  aguilera also performs a brand new song  loyal brave and  true written specially for the remake  and inspired by the virtues engraved on  mulan's sword  so what do you think about the new mulan  movie and how do you feel about the  changes  leave your thoughts in the comments  below if you enjoyed this then do leave  a thumbs up it really helps and i hugely  appreciate it  tap left for my next mulan video or tap  right for something else you're sure to  like  thanks for watching and see you next  time yippie kaio movie lovers  you

